# clinical v1.19 / health.shapes.ttl v1.7 — a resolved condition, stated in the
# declared clinical: spellings.
#
# WHAT THIS FIXTURE IS FOR. Two facts a condition record needs had declared
# homes that no shape named, while a reference import path wrote them under
# health:abatementDate and health:conditionCategory, predicates the health
# vocabulary does not define. clinical:abatementDate could not even be written
# here honestly before this release: it declared rdfs:domain clinical:Condition,
# a class no import path emits, and clinical v1.19 drops that domain so the
# property is writable on health:ConditionRecord, the class that IS emitted.
#
# THE TWO FACTS, AND WHY EACH VALUE:
#
#   clinical:abatementDate  "2025-06-30"^^xsd:date
#                           FHIR R4 Condition.abatement[x]. Written at DATE
#                           precision, the same sh:or over xsd:date and
#                           xsd:dateTime that health:onsetDate above it uses,
#                           because a resolution recorded as a calendar day must
#                           not be given an invented time. health:onsetDate on
#                           this same record is an xsd:dateTime, so the record
#                           carries one of each and a shape that had picked
#                           either branch alone would fail on one of them.
#   clinical:category       "problem-list-item"
#                           FHIR R4 Condition.category, extensible binding,
#                           which IPS marks must-support. Two codes exist and
#                           this is the one that means "carried on the patient's
#                           problem list", which is what a resolved chronic
#                           condition is. The other, "encounter-diagnosis", is
#                           what its sibling condition-category-diagnosis.WARN
#                           .ttl is about not being.
#
# health:status "resolved" and the abatement date are stated TOGETHER on
# purpose: the status says the condition ended and the abatement date says when,
# and a record carrying one without the other is the incomplete form this
# release lets a converter stop producing.
#
# The health: spellings are deliberately ABSENT here; the sibling
# condition-health-spelling.WARN.ttl carries them.
#
# Fully synthetic. No such patient exists.

@prefix cascade: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/core/v1#> .
@prefix clinical: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/clinical/v1#> .
@prefix health: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/health/v1#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<urn:uuid:c0nd0119-0000-4000-8000-000000000001> a health:ConditionRecord ;
    health:conditionName "Iron Deficiency Anemia" ;
    health:status "resolved" ;
    health:onsetDate "2023-11-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    clinical:abatementDate "2025-06-30"^^xsd:date ;
    clinical:category "problem-list-item" ;
    health:icd10Code <http://hl7.org/fhir/sid/icd-10-cm/D50.9> ;
    health:snomedCode <http://snomed.info/sct/87522002> ;
    health:conditionClass "hematologic" ;
    health:sourceRecordId "ehr-cond-2023-1104-001" ;
    cascade:dataProvenance cascade:EHRVerified ;
    cascade:schemaVersion "1.4" .
